<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The two DocLang media types, for shared-mime-info.

  The specification recommends `.dclg` for bare markup and `.dclx` for an
  archive, and names one media type: `application/vnd.doclang.document+xml`,
  which is what an archive's `[Content_Types].xml` gives `document.xml`. A bare
  `.dclg` is that same document with nothing round it, so it takes that type.

  The specification names no type for the archive itself. The name below is
  Segler's, chosen to sit beside the one the specification has, and it is
  provisional: if the DocLang project adopts a name for the archive, this file
  changes to it and the old name goes. Nothing else in this repository depends
  on the string.

  Neither type declares magic. An archive begins with the ZIP signature, which
  identifies every OPC package alike, and a bare document begins with an XML
  prolog or with `<doclang`, which a prolog pushes past the sniff window. The
  globs are the identification, as they are for every other OPC format.

  This file is the one declaration of the types on a Linux machine and ships in
  the `segler` package. Two packages cannot ship one path, so if a second
  DocLang product for Linux ever exists, the declaration and the icons move to
  a package both depend on, the way slipcase-common holds Slipcase's.

  Author: David M. Anderson
  Built with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic)
-->
<mime-info xmlns="http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/shared-mime-info">
  <mime-type type="application/vnd.doclang.archive+zip">
    <comment>DocLang archive</comment>
    <!--
    An archive is a ZIP, so an archive tool can open one and a desktop that
    knows nothing of DocLang still has something to offer. Where a name
    matches the glob and the content sniffs as `application/zip`,
    shared-mime-info takes the glob, because the magic-matched type is the
    parent rather than the child. The subclass carries no icon with it; the
    two icon elements below do that.
    -->
    <sub-class-of type="application/zip"/>
    <glob pattern="*.dclx"/>
    <icon name="application-vnd.doclang.archive+zip"/>
    <!--
    Named again as the generic icon, and the reason is how GTK searches. GIO
    hands the file manager the whole name list at once and GTK4 tries every
    name in Adwaita before any name in hicolor. The default generic icon for
    `application/*` is `application-x-generic`, which Adwaita has, so it wins
    every lookup and the hicolor drawing is never reached. Naming the specific
    icon here leaves Adwaita nothing to answer. slipcase-desktop measured it
    both ways.
    -->
    <generic-icon name="application-vnd.doclang.archive+zip"/>
  </mime-type>
  <mime-type type="application/vnd.doclang.document+xml">
    <comment>DocLang document</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="application/xml"/>
    <glob pattern="*.dclg"/>
    <icon name="application-vnd.doclang.document+xml"/>
    <generic-icon name="application-vnd.doclang.document+xml"/>
  </mime-type>
</mime-info>
